out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report – mediation (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['A clause that does everything the unit teaches, and a supplier who read it in February and said nothing. Asked whether the clause is wrong, Rim said no — she would write it again, and Mr Deeb is right as well, which is the part she had not expected. His account in his own words: a man who writes back in February saying your figure is too tight is a man who cannot do the work. And she asked four suppliers for comments and believed the silence meant agreement. So the mediation, in three moves: separate what the clause is for from what its reader could do about it; find the thing the document cannot do; and say whether the writer can supply it. The clause is for letting a driver and an inspector reach the same answer in two minutes at a gate, and it does that — eleven disputes down to one. The thing it cannot do, Jad said, is ask: a specification is written by the party who applies it and met by the party who cannot change it, and the twenty minutes of 21.2 is advice the writer can take and the supplier cannot ask for. Which is the finding of the cycle and it is new — everything in the unit has been addressed to the writer, and nothing in it tells a supplier how to say that a figure is tighter than his banding without sounding like a man who cannot do the work. Mazen said he has been on the other side of that and has never once written back to the exchange about a test card. Adnan said what she can supply is a line on the page itself: issue 2 is for comment until the thirtieth of April, and a comment on a figure will not be held against a tender. Seventeen words. And whose decision that is: hers, and only hers, because a supplier cannot make it safe to speak and the person who awards the work is the only person who can. Lubna asked what we must not do: do not widen the figure because somebody stayed silent, and do not ask for comments without saying what a comment costs.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **Mark the report on whether the supplier\'s silence is reported as reasonable rather than as a failure of nerve.** **The sentence to look for is his own:** _a man who writes back in February saying your figure is too tight is a man who cannot do the work._ **A version in which he should have spoken up has lost the whole cycle.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('⚠ _I read the silence **like** agreement._ → _as agreement_.   ⚠ _A comment will not be **hold** against a tender._ → _held_.   ⚠ _He had a **commercial** reason **for not to say**._ → _for not saying_.   ⚠ _The only person **who can** make it safe **are** the buyer._ → _is_.   ⚠ _**Tender** used for the document and the act_ — **keep** _tender_ **for the offer and** _specification_ **for the page.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read – exam practice (Reading and Use of English Part 7)'));
out.push(A('**1** **A**   **2** **C**   **3** **D**   **4** **B**   **5** **B**   **6** **C**'));
out.push(A('⚠ **Item 1 is the one to check against the text rather than against memory.** **A says the five lines are _neither stricter nor looser_, which is the exact answer** — **and students reach for B, because B talks about a revision and sounds like a change of standard.** ⚠ **Items 4 and 5 are both B and students distrust that**, **so teach the rubric once: _the people may be chosen more than once_ means exactly that, and an answer sheet with no repeats in a Part 7 of six questions is almost always wrong.** **Item 3 is the only one with a single candidate** — **D is the only account that gives a commercial reason for silence** — **and it is worth doing first, because it is the anchor.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
